    "content": {
      "value": "note\n\nThis page defines a small block of information that every page pushes about itself: what kind of page it is, which market it belongs to, and which currency it uses. This is a build item: Accenture builds this push into every page of the new site.\n\nThis page defines a small block of information that every page pushes about itself: what kind of page it is, which market it belongs to, and which currency it uses. This is a build item: Accenture builds this push into every page of the new site.\n\nEvery page announces what it is, so tracking never has to guess from the URL. This one habit keeps tagging and reporting simple and hard to break.\n\nEvery page pushes this before the tag manager loads:\n\njswindow.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];\ndataLayer.push({\npage: {\ntype: \"pdp\", // required, one of the allowed values below\nbreadcrumb: \"Swim > Capes\" // optional, the site hierarchy as displayed\n},\nsite: {\nmarket: \"AU\", // required, store market code\ncurrency: \"AUD\", // required, currency of the active store view\nenvironment: \"production\", // required: production or staging\nstore\\_view: \"AU Store View\" // required, the store view name\n}\n});\n\nAllowed values for page.type\n----------------------------\n\n`home`, `plp`, `search_results`, `pdp`, `cart`, `checkout`, `order_confirmation`, `account`, `content`, `store_locator`, `other`\n\nTwo of these are shorthand: `plp` is a product listing page, meaning a category page, and `pdp` is a product detail page, meaning a single product's page.\n\nThe list is closed. If a new page type is needed, it is added to this specification first. The build team does not invent new values.\n\nHow we will test it\n-------------------\n\n* The push appears before the tag manager on every page, in every market.\n* `page.type` always uses a value from the list above.\n* `site.currency` matches the currency used by ecommerce events on the same page.",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

